Z||000000||roadwarrior||08-30-2001||09:13 PM||||help, I can't figure what the size of my rear main brake line is on my 84 2m4se, it doesn't seem to be standard but all the part stores tell me that there is no metric line, only fittings,any insight would be much appreciated, thanx in advance.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Hank is Here||08-30-2001||09:16 PM||||Brake line sizes are measured by their outside diameter (OD) .  so just take a caliper and measure the diameter.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||roadwarrior||08-30-2001||09:25 PM||||I know how it is mesured but the problem is that it is inbetween sizes, and it does fit the old fittings<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||jelly2m8||08-30-2001||10:03 PM||||get 1/4 inch line for the main rear line, the rest are 3/16". If the line seems a bit too big to accept it, use the matching drill bit to make the fittings the right size.||||reg||1
Z||000004||SteveJ||08-31-2001||06:15 AM||||Everything was off the shelf at Autozone. the 1/4 line needs an adapter at the porp. valve in the front and at the "y" block over the pass rear wheel.||||reg||1
Z||000005||roadwarrior||08-31-2001||07:49 PM||||that's what I was thinking of doing jelly, but if I drill the fittings it throws the squish angles off, and those are the size of lines that I thought they were I have all the metric fittings and line but isn't there line 32nd of an inch?||||reg||1
Z||000006||SteveJ||08-31-2001||08:24 PM||||*mumbles*looks down at floor*clasps hands behind back*looks back up and then back down*starts walking in circles*mumbles in very low voice*. . .don't know why he doesn't just measure length of the line and take a section of it to the parts store. . .with the old fitting on the end. . . Let them figure out sizes. . .and fittings. . .and Autozone has that good green coating on the lines that prevents rust. . .drill??. . .what the he!!, drill???||||reg||1
Z||000007||SteveJ||08-31-2001||08:26 PM||||Pre formed steel line has a shorter fitting on one end and a longer one on the other end. Longer end goes into the proportioning valve I beleive.||||reg||1
Z||000008||jelly2m8||08-31-2001||09:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by roadwarrior:<BR><B>that's what I was thinking of doing jelly, but if I drill the fittings it throws the squish angles off, and those are the size of lines that I thought they were I have all the metric fittings and line but isn't there line 32nd of an inch?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Everytime theres even the slightest mention of modifying a part, someone always jumps in and gets worried about a questionable outcome of that part. Theres 2 things when considering this, its Theory and Real world. 9 times out of 10, what sounds impossible in theory usually works in the real world. <BR> Lets look at the Grand Am brake conversion for a second. In theory it doesnt sound so good, but in the real world the GA brakes work great on a Fiero.<P> On the part about drilling the fitting, hardly any material comes out of a fitting if you drill the main rear line fitting with a 1/4 inch drill bit, for example. I never measured the difference, and have never met anyone who did. Theres simply no need to. Ask your local GM tech what he uses to replace brake lines. <P> Heres one that is surely going to sit bad with some folks, on one of my Fieros the bradied line at the slave cylinder let go at the fitting. The simple solution was to get a rear flex hose for a chrysler minivan. But that flex hose is only 3/16", where as the clutch line is (oh my god!!!) 1/4"...<BR>What i did was use 3/16" flarenuts that fit the flex hose ( God bless Chrysler for sticking with standard sized tubing), and drilled them out to 1/4". Nasty you think?, well this has been on the car for the past 7 years without any problems.<P> Well I'm off to drive my Fiero with the drilled brake fittings,  the Grand Am brakes, solid tranny mounts, and Lumina inner tierods on the rear......||||reg||1
